# Media Pack Maintenance

When new part images are imported or uploaded to the `media/` folder, the media pack must be rebuilt so new installs get the latest images.

## After importing or adding images

1. Run `build_media_pack.bat` to regenerate `dist/ApexDatabase_Media.zip`
2. The zip contains the full `media/` folder (35k+ files, ~7.5 GB uncompressed)
3. Copy the updated zip to the USB drive alongside the installer

## How the media pack works

- `launcher.py`  `ensure_media()` checks if `%LOCALAPPDATA%\ApexDatabase\media\` has files
- If empty (fresh install): searches for `ApexDatabase_Media.zip` on USB drives (D:-Z:) and the install directory
- If found: extracts all images automatically on first launch
- If already populated (update): skips entirely - existing images are preserved

## Key files

- `build_media_pack.bat` / `build_media_pack.py` - builds the zip
- `launcher.py` - `ensure_media()` handles detection and extraction
- `data_import/management/commands/export_seed_data.py` - `catalog_partimage` is included in the seed so image metadata ships with the database

## Pre-converting JPEGs to PNG for the Qt (ChoreBoy) edition

The Qt edition of Apex Database that runs on the Linux box "ChoreBoy" has a broken Qt JPEG plugin and can only render PNGs. To ship a single `ApexDatabase_Media.zip` that works for BOTH the Windows Django app and the Qt edition, the build can pre-convert JPEGs to PNG before zipping. This is opt-in - default behaviour is unchanged.

### How to opt in

Pass `--preconvert` to `build_media_pack.bat`, or set the `MEDIA_PRECONVERT` env var before invoking it:

```powershell
build_media_pack.bat --preconvert siblings
# or
$env:MEDIA_PRECONVERT = "siblings"; build_media_pack.bat
```

The flag is forwarded to `tools/preconvert_media.py`, which can also be invoked directly:

```powershell
.\venv\Scripts\python.exe -u tools\preconvert_media.py --source media --mode siblings
.\venv\Scripts\python.exe -u tools\preconvert_media.py --source media --mode replace
.\venv\Scripts\python.exe -u tools\preconvert_media.py --source media --mode export-only --dest dist\media_png
```

### Option A vs Option B

- **`siblings`** (Option A, **safe and recommended**) - writes a `.png` next to every `.jpg`/`.jpeg` in `media/`. Originals are kept, so every existing Django `ImageField` row still points at a file that exists. The zip is larger but the Windows install keeps working with no other changes. The Qt-side resolver picks up the `.png` sibling automatically.
- **`replace`** (Option B, **needs an audit first**) - writes the `.png` and deletes the original JPEG. Smaller / faster zip, but every Django consumer that opens files by the DB-stored path (which still says `.jpg`/`.jpeg`) will 404 unless a resolver is added or a DB migration rewrites `catalog_part.image`, `catalog_partimage.image`, `catalog_unit.unit_image`, `catalog_unit.plug_image`, `catalog_unitimage.image`. Do not use without re-running the consumer audit.
- **`export-only`** - copies the tree into a separate `--dest` and never mutates `media/`. Useful when you want to build a Qt-targeted zip without touching the live folder.

### Key files

- `tools/preconvert_media.py` - the standalone converter (uses Pillow, already in `requirements.txt` and `setup_cx_freeze.py` PACKAGES)
- `build_media_pack.py` - exposes the `--preconvert {none,siblings,replace}` flag and forwards to the converter
- `build_media_pack.bat` - forwards extra args (`%*`) to `build_media_pack.py`